      "completion_tokens": 1055,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**, which means it is made up of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. The Molecular Structure\nWater molecules are polar, meaning they have a positive end and a negative end. The oxygen atom is slightly negative, and the two hydrogen atoms are slightly positive. This polarity allows water molecules to stick to one another (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion), which is why water forms droplets and flows.\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is the only common substance on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, liquid, and gas at the temperatures normally found on the planet:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules move slowly and form a rigid crystal lattice.\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we use for drinking and washing. It flows and takes the shape of its container.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Water Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move very fast and spread out into the air.\n\n### 3. Unique Properties\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Water is excellent at dissolving other substances. This is why it is essential for biological processes; it helps transport nutrients and waste inside living organisms.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water has a strong \"skin\" on its surface, which allows small insects to walk on ponds without sinking.\n*   **Density Anomaly:** Unlike most materials, water is less dense as a solid (ice) than as a liquid. This is why ice floats on water, insulating lakes and oceans in winter to keep aquatic life alive.\n\n### 4. Importance to Life\nWater is the foundation of life on Earth. It is a major component of all living cells (about 60% of the human body) and is necessary for:\n*   Digestion and nutrient absorption.\n*   Regulating body temperature (sweating).\n*   Lubricating joints and eyes.\n*   Removing waste through urination and sweating."
